--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/dashboard-profile.md
@@ -0,0 +1,93 @@
+# Bounded larger-report and multiple-node profile
+
+3 October 2026. A local synthetic telemetry profile passes through the real
+collector, PostgreSQL and HTTP API with eight authenticated identities, each
+reporting a 714-transaction head and fifty-block history. This tests dashboard
+transport/storage, not executed blocks, independent operators or chain throughput.
+
+The final profile sends two eight-node history batches, one before and one during
+snapshot persistence, then advances every head through heights 1000–1003. Each
+history reply is 2,811,901–2,811,902 application bytes; head reports reach 56,283
+bytes. Distinct versions, peer/pending counts, current-head hashes and forty
+aligned transaction-count chart entries survive storage/API. The current-head
+transaction lists remain complete on both `/nodes` and `/blocks`.
+
+| Fixture setting | Value | Scope |
+| --- | ---: | --- |
+| Collector input | 4,194,304 bytes | Strict incoming message threshold |
+| Collector pending output | 1,048,576 bytes | Per TCP connection |
+| Snapshot frame/message | 1,048,576 bytes | Persistence receiver |
+| Snapshot node count | 8 | Exact enrolled test roster |
+| Collector connections | 16 | Shared across paths; not the enrollment count |
+| Telemetry rate | 100 messages/s | Per application connection |
+| Registration deadline | 5,000 ms | Fixture |
+| Snapshot interval / deadline | 250 / 1,500 ms | Fixture |
+| Snapshot / node-report lifetime | 5,000 / 30,000 ms | Fixture |
+
+These values are a measured candidate profile, not deployment defaults. All
+services are loopback-only. The final run samples about 238.5 MiB collector RSS
+at its highest observed point, with startup already about 126.9 MiB. Sampling
+includes message hooks and a 25 ms timer, not continuous peak measurement. The
+fixture parses messages for size/event instrumentation and adds overhead. No
+production RAM budget can be inferred from this short run or by summing sampled
+component maxima. PostgreSQL, driver and browser memory are separate.
+
+Two controlled cases establish why budgets must be coordinated:
+
+- With two busy identities, a 114,210-byte inventory exceeds the 64 KiB receiver
+  budget and closes with code 1009. Nothing is saved; HTTP returns 503.
+- With eight, a roughly 457 kB inventory exceeds the 256 KiB collector output
+  budget. The guarded write closes the connection (client code 1006). Again,
+  nothing is saved; HTTP returns 503.
+
+The successful run's largest inventory is 457,084 bytes, stored snapshot JSON
+461,205 bytes and `/nodes` response 483,281 bytes. `/blocks` is 474,540 bytes and
+`/charts` 5,700 bytes. Four successful saves take 12.7–17.1 ms in this run.
+Both large history batches finish their telemetry acknowledgements within
+292 ms; later head-only batches within 24 ms. These are observed local timings,
+not guarantees. Controlled clock advancement after stopping ingestion verifies
+HTTP expiry; it is not a real-time outage or mining-continuity experiment.
+
+The profile exposed silent transport failures: the receiver can close before
+the application sees a payload, without calling the writer's `onError`. The
+small production change in `wsclient/collect-snapshots.js` reports unexpected
+closes and socket errors through that existing callback. `wsclient.js` already
+logs it. Repeated callbacks and error-then-close produce one diagnostic; explicit
+stop and previously reported failures do not add a second message. Raw close
+reasons/exception messages are not logged. Reconnection, storage and freshness
+rules stay unchanged. External alert routing is still separate G4 work.
+
+The two new diagnostic regressions fail on the preceding production source.
+The candidate passes 161 contracts, 12 socket tests, six database scenarios,
+both constrained profile cases and the final eight-node profile. The earlier
+profile attempt waited for an error callback that did not exist; its preserved
+failure led to the close-event diagnostics and this correction.
+
+## Admission and deployment review
+
+Current source still accepts arbitrary object-shaped `hello.info` metadata and
+copies unknown current-head extensions. `node-ping.clientTime` is echoed without
+a separate type/byte bound. All remain subject to transport/output guards, but
+4 MiB transport input does not promise that eight accepted heads plus metadata
+will fit a 1 MiB snapshot. The node cap is checked by persistence after receipt;
+TCP connection limits do not bound enrolled identities or retained inactive nodes.
+
+Before adopting a deployment profile, bound/project registration fields and ping
+values, and choose a documented per-head/extension policy while retaining the
+required transaction hashes. Calculate and test aggregate inventory size at the
+maximum roster, including inactive nodes, stats/history envelopes, serialization
+and chart/output headroom. Coordinate credential enrollment and snapshot node
+limits. Then test the selected proxy/TLS route with the same workload and choose
+memory, retry, polling and alert thresholds. Keep `/primus` persistence access
+private as required by the deployment design.
+
+This profile uses common reported history: 54 retained heights/variants after
+advancement. Combine its scope with the separate 18,000-variant offline cache
+experiment; neither is a soak, slow-public-network or public-user fan-out test.
+No efsn, consensus, schema, dependency or frontend changes are included.
+
+Review artifacts: node repository `docs/evidence/restart-dashboard-profile-2026-10-03/`.
+Run `node --test test/dashboard-profile.test.cjs` only with an explicit
+`DASHBOARD_PROFILE`, `DASHBOARD_PROFILE_EVIDENCE`, `TEST_PG_PORT` and
+`TEST_PG_PASSWORD_FILE` for a fresh disposable database. Profiles are
+`small-snapshot`, `small-output` and `aligned`.
